     GRANT com.google.android.gms THE ONE PRIVILEGED PERMISSION MindTheGapps OMITS.

     Measured on build 72, 93 records in a single boot:

       E DatabaseUtils: java.lang.SecurityException: Permission denial:
         writing to settings requires:android.permission.WRITE_DEVICE_CONFIG
           at ...SettingsProvider.enforceWritePermission(SettingsProvider.java:2314)
           at ...SettingsProvider.setAllConfigSettings(SettingsProvider.java:1173)
       W bbin (com.google.android.gms.persistent):
           at android.provider.DeviceConfig.setProperties(DeviceConfig.java:980)
           at ...gms.platformconfigurator.PhenotypeConfigurationUpdateListener.onHandleIntent

     That is Phenotype, Google's server-driven flag system, pushing DeviceConfig
     properties into the platform. Every push failed, so no flag ever applied.

     WHY IT FAILED, and why it is not a bug in this tree:
       GmsCore IS correctly privileged — /product/priv-app/GmsCore/GmsCore.apk.
       WRITE_DEVICE_CONFIG is signature|privileged, so a privileged app only gets
       it when an allowlist grants it. MindTheGapps ships
       /product/etc/permissions/privapp-permissions-google-product.xml with 95
       permissions for this package and WRITE_DEVICE_CONFIG is not among them:
         dumpsys package com.google.android.gms
           android.permission.WRITE_DEVICE_CONFIG: granted=false
       GMS requests it; nothing grants it.

     WHY A SEPARATE FILE INSTEAD OF EDITING THEIRS:
       SystemConfig MERGES privapp-permissions blocks across files rather than
       letting one win — frameworks/base/.../SystemConfig.java:1601 fetches the
       existing ArraySet for the package, :1616 adds to it, :1630 puts it back. So
       this file ADDS to MindTheGapps' 95 and leaves their curation intact. Editing
       a third-party vendor package would fork it for one line and lose the change
       on their next drop.

     🔴 IT MUST LIVE ON /product. The privapp allowlist is read per partition and
     must sit on the same one as the app; GmsCore.apk is in /product/priv-app, so
     a copy in /system/etc/permissions would be parsed and then not apply.

     ⚠ THIS IS A POLICY CHOICE, NOT A DEFECT FIX. It lets Google set platform
     DeviceConfig flags remotely, which is exactly what a Google-certified device
     allows — consistent with this ROM presenting the honest stock identity.
     MindTheGapps may well omit it deliberately, aiming at a smaller Google
     footprint. Operator decision, 2026-08-20. To reverse it, delete this file and
     the PRODUCT_COPY_FILES line in device.mk; nothing else references it.
-->
<permissions>
    <privapp-permissions package="com.google.android.gms">
        <permission name="android.permission.WRITE_DEVICE_CONFIG"/>
    </privapp-permissions>
</permissions>
